WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.380
Speaker 1: Hey, does cancel culture actually exist&nbsp;
in light of the latest right wing cancellation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:13.320
the new Eminem's? We have to talk about cancel&nbsp;
culture. Does cancel culture actually exist?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:21.600
Someone sent me an article from the Center on&nbsp;
a Stateless Society, which is sort of the basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:28.860
for this segment. And that article really got me&nbsp;
thinking. And what I mean by does cancel culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:35.760
really exist is sometimes things are canceled&nbsp;
in the sense that people are fired or a show is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:43.380
canceled or someone is ostracized or people change&nbsp;
their views, or activists express opinions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:49.800
say, I don't like what you said. That happens,&nbsp;
right? That takes place. But does the cancel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:58.620
culture that Republicans describe actually exist?&nbsp;
Does this culture exist when Republicans scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:05.340
we are being canceled by the woke left? I&nbsp;
believe that it does not really exist. Often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:11.100
what is happening is that someone's words&nbsp;
or actions are having consequences as those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.200
words and actions are being evaluated in the&nbsp;
free market of ideas which Republicans claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:23.400
to support. Oftentimes the canceled still have a&nbsp;
bully pulpit, plenty of access to corporate media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:30.300
social media and other platforms to spread their&nbsp;
views. Sometimes they're canceled in the sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.860
that they lose when they're entertainers. They&nbsp;
lose one opportunity, but then they come back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:40.620
and they get another opportunity. And sometimes&nbsp;
it takes a little while. You look at Mel Gibson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:45.000
who had this horrible incident where he was pulled&nbsp;
over drunk driving, and he said to the officer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.960
Are you a Jew? And I guess the officer&nbsp;
was Jewish. You know, what are the odds?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.960
And then he, you know, made all sorts of other&nbsp;
horrible statements. And there was a period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.920
during which Mel Gibson wasn't doing much work&nbsp;
in Hollywood, but like, then he's back. Then he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.460
started getting work again. And so is that&nbsp;
is that really a cancellation? There was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:08.040
period during which his statements and actions&nbsp;
impacted his career. It seems logical that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.720
such a free market, there are consequences&nbsp;
to the things that you say and that you do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:19.440
And so often and this is really what I believe&nbsp;
is at the crux of this meme of cancel culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.960
that the right likes to talk about. Often those&nbsp;
who are accused of doing the canceling are people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:31.260
from groups that only more recently are getting a&nbsp;
louder voice. They're from previously marginalized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:37.020
groups. And thanks to social media and the&nbsp;
Internet, some of those individuals, some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:42.360
those groups are now having more of a voice to&nbsp;
express their views. People speak out and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:48.420
push back against marginalization, and sometimes&nbsp;
there are consequences to the person doing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:55.200
marginalizing. And now they have been canceled as&nbsp;
if it's bad that there are consequences for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:01.380
things that they have said or done. Now, it's&nbsp;
also worth noting that by their own definition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:08.460
of canceling, it is often Republicans that are&nbsp;
doing the canceling, despite the fact that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.260
say it's the left that does the canceling. And&nbsp;
we see this in the way that they try to silence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:19.320
dissenting voices in the media and in academia and&nbsp;
in politics. They want to control the narrative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:24.000
They want to shut down voices that challenge&nbsp;
their ideology. And when they get called out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:30.600
they say it's cancel culture as a way to deflect&nbsp;
responsibility and to play the victim everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.820
they accuse the left of doing, they do. Let&nbsp;
me remind you of something else. This isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:40.140
as popular in the last couple of years,&nbsp;
but you used to often hear from the right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:47.700
Saying you're offended isn't an argument. And&nbsp;
I agreed with them. Right. If someone makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:53.760
a statement and someone on the left or the&nbsp;
right responds by saying that's offensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.940
Boom. Conversation over. I think that&nbsp;
that is a way to shut down an argument,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:05.460
to shut down debate. And it's not actually&nbsp;
an argument in and of itself. You offended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:09.180
me. Boom. Well, let's let's talk about what&nbsp;
I said. Let's talk about white. White. Okay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:17.700
That's cancel culture is exactly the same thing.&nbsp;
It is exactly equivalent to I'm offended. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:22.440
conversation that's cancel culture. That's it.&nbsp;
We're done. It's exactly the same thing now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:30.900
I'll be upfront with everybody here. You all&nbsp;
know I am not blind to the reality that sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:38.580
people may go too far in their activism. Sometimes&nbsp;
the calls for someone's head are premature or they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:44.640
are inappropriate. If we go back and think&nbsp;
about, by the way, when Democrats demanded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:51.720
that their own Senator, Al Franken, resign. Al&nbsp;
Franken participated in a tasteless joke that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:59.640
was photographed and it was tasteless. There's no&nbsp;
question about it. But in a sense, Democrats were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:04.740
too quick to say you got to resign Al Franken,&nbsp;
partially because Democrats wanted to show how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:10.260
how fair and nonpartisan they were when it came&nbsp;
to these things. And the reality is that what Al&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:15.300
Franken did was really quite minor compared to&nbsp;
the things others have gotten away with. So I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:20.520
not blind to the fact that sometimes people go too&nbsp;
far, but that doesn't mean you can't hold anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:26.700
accountable. And if you do, it's cancel culture.&nbsp;
So we need to shift from calling people out to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.560
It's so cliche like sometimes that people say&nbsp;
we need to call people in to talk. Right. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:41.160
is all the reality of there are now many&nbsp;
more platforms on which people can speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:48.660
and there are many more platforms on which people&nbsp;
can respond to speech. And so that's the reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:54.420
And so the idea of cancel culture as this&nbsp;
monolithic movement to salespeople is basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:59.760
meaningless. It's not accurately representing&nbsp;
what's happening. There's no conspiracy. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:07.920
the marketplace of ideas that the right holds&nbsp;
up as a place where ideas or jokes or bigotry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:13.560
or ad campaigns or content or whatever are open&nbsp;
to criticism, and they're open to consequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:18.060
So let's get away from the term cancel culture.&nbsp;
I use it tongue in cheek when I say, look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:24.060
it's cancel culture from the right, but it's&nbsp;
really a political boogeyman and it's meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:29.940
silence voices, often marginalized voices. It's&nbsp;
meant to protect the status quo, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:35.460
when it comes to Republicans by making them&nbsp;
immune to the consequences of their actions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:41.220
and their words. Don't fall for it. Let's actually&nbsp;
push back against the concept of cancel culture.

